# PREDICT THE OUTPUT

### Given Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Engine
{
    Engine()
    {
        cout << "Engine ";
    }

    ~Engine()
    {
        cout << "~Engine ";
    }
};

struct Vehicle
{
    Vehicle()
    {
        cout << "Vehicle ";
    }

    ~Vehicle()
    {
        cout << "~Vehicle ";
    }
};

struct Car : Vehicle
{
    Engine e;

    Car()
    {
        cout << "Car ";
    }

    ~Car()
    {
        cout << "~Car ";
    }
};

int main()
{
    {
        Car c;
        cout << "| ";
    }

    cout << '\n';
}
```

### Predicted Output

```text
Vehicle Engine Car | ~Car ~Engine ~Vehicle
```

### Explanation

- Vehicle is constructed first because it is the base class.
- Engine is constructed next because it is a member object.
- Car constructor executes last.
- When the object is destroyed, Car destructor executes first.
- Then Engine is destroyed.
- Finally, Vehicle is destroyed.

**Rule:** Base classes are constructed first, followed by member objects in declaration order, and then the derived class. Destruction occurs in the reverse order.

