## Swap the Initializer List Order and Compile with -Wall

Change the constructor to:

```cpp
Student(const string& n, int a, long long r,
        const string& c, int p)
    : home(c, p), regNo(r), Person(n, a)
{
}
```

Compile using:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o main
```

### Warning

The compiler may display a warning similar to:

```text
warning: 'Student::home' will be initialized after
warning: 'Student::regNo'
warning: when initialized here
```

The exact warning wording may differ depending on the compiler version.

### Explanation

C++ does not initialize members according to the order written in the initializer list.

The actual initialization order is:

1. Base class Person
2. Member regNo
3. Member home

The order is determined by the order of declaration inside the class, not by the initializer list. Therefore, changing the initializer list order can produce a -Wreorder warning.

The output remains the same:

```text
192311001 Kavya (19) Salem-636001
```

