# ALTERNATIVE CONCEPT AND COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

## Using a Pointer Instead of a Member Object

Instead of storing an Address object directly:

```cpp
Address home;
```

Student can store a pointer:

```cpp
Address* home;
```

### Alternative Code

```cpp
class Student : public Person
{
    long long regNo;
    Address* home;

public:
    Student(const string& n, int a, long long r,
            const string& c, int p)
        : Person(n, a), regNo(r)
    {
        home = new Address(c, p);
    }

    void print() const
    {
        cout << regNo << ' ' << name << " (" << age << ") "
             << home->str() << '\n';
    }

    ~Student()
    {
        delete home;
    }
};
```

### Comparison

| Feature | Address Member Object | Address Pointer |
|---|---|---|
| Storage | Address stored directly inside Student | Pointer stored inside Student |
| Memory allocation | No separate dynamic allocation | Dynamic allocation using new |
| Time | Faster and simpler | Extra allocation and deallocation time |
| Space | Stores the Address object | Stores a pointer plus dynamically allocated Address |
| Destructor | Automatically destroys Address | Must delete the allocated Address |
| Copy constructor | Automatic copying works | Must perform deep copy |
| Memory leak risk | Low | Possible if delete is forgotten |

### Complexity

- Both approaches require O(1) time to access the address.
- Member object approach requires O(1) space.
- Pointer approach also requires O(1) space, but additionally allocates memory dynamically.
- Dynamic allocation and deallocation add extra overhead.

### Conclusion

Using a member object is simpler, safer and more efficient when every Student always has an Address. Using a pointer is useful when the Address may be optional or needs to be created dynamically, but it requires careful memory management, a proper copy constructor and destructor.
